Two strings you can play now, and a third worth stringing. Poincaré is the established reference. As of November 24, Levent Alpöge’s Lean proof of the Riemann hypothesis, developed with Claude, has earned public confidence from the Lean FRO and Mathlib teams. Clay still lists it as active. Clay also said in September that Navier–Stokes appears to have been settled, so there’s another candidate for the guitar. 

String	What it lets us listen for
Poincaré	Shape. What stays true when you continuously deform a space?
Riemann	Rhythm. How do the primes wander around their expected distribution?
Navier–Stokes	Motion. Can a flow that starts smooth develop a singularity?

Your vertical-versus-horizontal thought gives us a way to play the Riemann string. The hypothesis pins the real part of every nontrivial zero to 1/2. In the usual plot, that fixes the horizontal coordinate. The vertical coordinates still vary, and you can use those heights as frequencies in a sonification. One coordinate is fixed, and the other supplies the notes. 

The compounding happens in the chords. Two strings give you one pair. Three strings give you three pairs and a trio. If you count every subset of two or more strings as a possible chord, seven strings give you 120 combinations. That’s exponential growth in possible questions. The useful ones come from finding a relationship that survives the translation.

I’d play Poincaré + Riemann first. Use the same prime-derived notes to trace a closed path on a sphere and around a torus. On the torus, send the path once around the hole. Then try to shrink both loops to a point while staying on their surfaces. Same tune, different fate. That gives the Pantheon a four-word sketch it can reopen as a math lesson, a visual, or a story about two travelers who follow the same directions through worlds with different rules.